= Ecosystem =

== Overview ==
An '''ecosystem''' comprises a community of living organisms, their interactions, and the nonliving physical and chemical environment with which they exchange energy and matter. It can refer to a small pond or tide pool, a forest, a coastal marine area or a broader region under a stated spatial and temporal boundary.

== Living and nonliving components ==
Living components include producers, consumers, decomposers and other organisms in interacting populations. Nonliving components include water, temperature, light, soil, gases, minerals and disturbances. The importance of each component depends on the system and research question.

== Energy flow and nutrient cycling ==
Primary producers such as photosynthetic plants and algae capture energy and assimilate inorganic carbon. Organisms consume, transform, respire or decompose organic matter. '''Energy''' is transferred through trophic interactions and increasingly dissipated as heat; it is not endlessly recycled within a food web. In contrast, '''matter''' and many nutrients can move through repeated biological and geochemical cycles, including the [[Related to::Carbon cycle|carbon cycle]].

== Food webs, networks and boundaries ==
A food chain is a simplified path through energy transfer; actual food webs contain multiple interacting routes and changing species interactions. [[Related to::Complex system|Complex-system models]] can capture some of these interactions, but a large network diagram by itself does not establish causal dynamics. A chosen [[Related to::System boundary|system boundary]] determines which organisms, inputs, outputs and perturbations are included.

== Responses and change ==
Ecosystems are dynamic and may change after disturbances such as storms, fire, introduced species, harvesting or climate shifts. Recovery is not guaranteed to reproduce a previous state, and stability may refer to different measured properties. An observation of changing species abundance needs sampling and historical context before it can support claims about ecosystem-wide collapse or resilience.

== Scientific assessment ==
Ecosystem research may measure species diversity, primary production, nutrient fluxes, habitat conditions or interaction networks. A claim about net carbon uptake must account for respiration, losses and measurement uncertainty rather than equating photosynthesis with long-term storage.

